UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 37, Lesson 1: Choosing a research topic', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 students learn choosing a research topic through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice. Identify purpose and audience, apply a clear process, check work against criteria and explain choices with textual or factual support. Keep school-safe topics; credit sources; do not invent NESA content points beyond catalogue codes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou are practising choosing a research topic. State the purpose, list two criteria and complete one guided example with a because-explanation.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise. Weak answers skip criteria or invent unsupported claims.\n\nCHECK\nThinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a substantial task on choosing a research topic for a real classroom audience: plan, draft or perform as required, include evidence or sources where relevant, self-check against three criteria and write a five-sentence reflection naming one strength and one next goal.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Year 7 students learn choosing a research topic through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice. Identify purpose and audience, apply a clear process, check work against criteria and explain choices with textual or factual support. Keep school-safe topics; credit sources; do not invent NESA content points beyond catalogue codes.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading and viewing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a substantial task on choosing a research topic for a real classroom audience: plan, draft or perform as required, include evidence or sources where relevant, self-check against three criteria and  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using choosing a research topic on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck.. Correction: Strong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating choosing a research topic on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with choosing a research topic, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about choosing a research topic with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for choosing a research topic. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for choosing a research topic. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise"],"question":"You are practising choosing a research topic. State the purpose, list two criteria and complete one guided example with a because-explanation. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for choosing a research topic; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck","Year 7 students learn choosing a research topic through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice","Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise","Careful use of evidence and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with choosing a research topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named evidence and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name evidence and explain why it matters only, without linking it to choosing a research topic","Apply choosing a research topic to new material and name the evidence and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck"],"question":"Which response best applies choosing a research topic with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries choosing a research topic. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words","Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck","Point to exact words in the sentence that show choosing a research topic and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event"],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses choosing a research topic from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name evidence and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise","Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about choosing a research topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse evidence and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for choosing a research topic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new","Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about choosing a research topic","Retype the worked model as the final answer"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of choosing a research topic on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about choosing a research topic with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating choosing a research topic on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using choosing a research topic on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for choosing a research topic. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with choosing a research topic, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking choosing a research topic is only talent or first-draft luck.. Correction: Strong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w37-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise choosing a research topic.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted 